CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ empty vector has size 0: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^.*is unknown.*$
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
[temp.inst]/1-2 with [dcl.typedef]: a member typedef declares the member and
forms the aliased type but does not require the aliased template's definition to
be instantiated; a typedef-name may denote an incomplete type.

Instantiating std::vector<int> elaborates the self-referential member typedefs
`iterator`/`const_iterator` (= __normal_iterator<pointer/const_pointer,
vector>), whose type names the still-incomplete enclosing `vector`.  They cannot
be eagerly instantiated at that point; the self-reference branch of
typecheck_compound_body now keeps them as lazy aliases (registered by name)
rather than dropping them, so the sibling typedef
`const_reverse_iterator = reverse_iterator<const_iterator>` and other uses
resolve `const_iterator`, and the empty vector verifies genuinely (the assertion
is present in --show-properties and checked).

